## Background

Regional species inventories are a basic product of biodiversity research, and among the
hardest to reuse. They are typically published as tables in a PDF: names in a
column, localities as headers, and a legend of codes saying where each record comes from.
That layout is efficient for a reader and opaque to software. It also fixes the taxonomy at
the date of publication: when a name is later synonymised, the inventory cannot say so, and
aggregators that ingest it later can only match the names it printed. The FAIR principles
{{ cite("wilkinson2016") }} ask for data that are findable, accessible, interoperable and
reusable by machines as well as people. For legacy inventories the gap between the
published table and that goal is mostly data engineering: extraction, harmonisation,
identifiers and provenance.

The community standards for closing this gap exist. Darwin Core {{ cite("wieczorek2012") }}
provides the shared vocabulary for occurrences, taxa and locations, and Darwin-SW
{{ cite("baskauf2016") }} supplies the relationships between them that Darwin Core leaves
undefined. Taxonomic databases such as the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS)
{{ cite("worms2026") }}, the GBIF Backbone Taxonomy {{ cite("gbif2026") }} and NCBI Taxonomy
{{ cite("schoch2020") }} provide persistent identifiers for names. The Environment Ontology
(ENVO) {{ cite("buttigieg2013", "buttigieg2016") }} does the same for habitats. Mappings between
local and external identifiers can be published with their justification using SSSOM
{{ cite("matentzoglu2022") }}, and provenance can be expressed with PROV-O
{{ cite("w3c_provo") }}. Shape constraints in SHACL {{ cite("w3c_shacl") }} can check the
resulting graph. What is less often shown is how to apply these pieces together to one
published inventory, so that every statement in the graph can be traced back to the table cell
it came from, no value is invented, and the reuser learns how much each record can bear.

Here we take the inventory of shallow-water sea anemones sensu lato (Actiniaria,
Corallimorpharia, Ceriantharia and Zoantharia) of Venezuela {{ cite("gonzalezmunoz2016") }} as a
test case. The article does not define "shallow-water" by a depth: its species accounts give
depth ranges, which were not extracted here, and its Table 1 includes one species recorded at
4000–5000 m (*Monactis vestita*, in a column for the open sea); we therefore call the taxa
"coastal". The present author is one of its co-authors. The article combines three kinds of
evidence in {{ s.pages }} pages:

- a distribution matrix of {{ s.t1_taxa }} names across {{ s.t1_localities }} localities, with
  codes for the authors' surveys, museum specimens and {{ (s.t1_sources - 1) | words }} earlier publications;
- {{ s.t2 }} specimens collected in the study;
- {{ s.t3 }} specimens of the Museo Oceanológico Hermano Benigno Román (MOHBR) collection that
  the authors re-examined.

It is open access under CC BY 4.0, and its data are dedicated to the public domain (CC0).

Our aims were:

1. to extract all of its tables, and the name, authority and habitat of each species account,
   from the published PDF without manual transcription, and to check the extraction
   independently;
2. to harmonise them to a Darwin Core–aligned canonical model while keeping every value as
   published;
3. to resolve names and habitats to persistent identifiers, recording how each match was
   made;
4. to check data quality as code, and to grade the evidence behind each record, before
   building an RDF knowledge graph;
5. to validate the graph with SHACL and query it with SPARQL;

and to make the build deterministic, so that the same inputs (the PDF, the API cache, the
lockfile and the Poppler version) reproduce every output byte for byte (Fig. 1).

