## Methods

### Source data and the author's relationship to it

The publisher's PDF {{ cite("gonzalezmunoz2016") }} is the only input. Its SHA-256 checksum is
recorded in a manifest, and a test fails if the file changes. Table 1 lists the sources
in the article and what was extracted from each. The rest of each species account
(distribution text, remarks, depth) was not extracted. The legend of Table 1 reads: "(\*) records
documented in our surveys, (?) doubtful previous records, (†) species currently considered as
invalid, (N) new records for Venezuela. Citation of previous records: (a) Specimens of the MOHBR
scientific collection (see Appendix 2), (b) den Hartog (1980), […] (j) Carrasquel (2012)". Code
"a" is thus a *previous* record: a specimen deposited earlier by other collectors, which the
authors re-examined. Code "\*" does not promise a specimen from that area. The Methods say that
"Representative specimens of each species were collected", that "Specimens were photographed in
situ", and that "Voucher specimens of each identified species were deposited in the collection of
the MOHBR": vouchers were deposited per species, not per area. The survey consisted of
"Sampling […] in November 2012 in four marine reserves" and "observations and collections made in
Isla de Cubagua […] and Chichiriviche de la Costa"; effort is not reported. The museum specimens
include "those from Agudo (1987) vouchered at the MOHBR".

The author of this paper is a co-author of the source article and made every curation
decision described below. No independent taxonomist has yet audited them; they are published
in full, with their evidence, for that purpose (Additional file 3). Every value in the dataset
comes from the PDF; where the PDF is silent (for example, on the collection dates of the Isla de
Cubagua material) the value is left empty.

### Extraction and its checks

The tables have no column rules, so a line-based table finder cannot read them. Extraction
works on word bounding boxes obtained with pdfplumber {{ cite("sw_pdfplumber") }}:

- **Columns** are recovered from the gaps between header words. Words within a column header
  are separated by at most 3 pt, and columns by at least 6 pt.
- **Rows** are anchored on the row numbers printed in the margin.
- **Bold headers** in Table 1 are read from the font name; they mark the {{ s.t1_sampled | words }}
  localities the authors visited.

Table 1 is printed sideways and in two blocks of columns that repeat the same name rows. The
parser requires the two blocks to agree on every name. Extraction is *verbatim*: typing errors,
impossible dates and blank cells are kept, and every row carries its page and a locator such as
"Table 2, row 25". The parser raises an error instead of guessing whenever the sequence of row
numbers breaks, a cell contains a code that is not in the legend, or the number of columns is
unexpected; a test provokes each of these errors.

Three further checks look at every value, as a census rather than a sample, and any
disagreement stops the pipeline:

- **A second automated extraction** uses a different PDF engine (Poppler's
  `pdftotext`, instead of pdfminer under pdfplumber) and a separate parser. A Table 1 code
  agrees when both put it in the same row and column. A field of Tables 2 and 3 agrees when the
  text printed inside that column's band of the same row equals the extracted value, blanks
  included, so a value that slid into a neighbouring field fails. The two extractions share only
  layout knowledge: the page ranges, rows anchored on printed row numbers, the two column
  blocks of Table 1, the code set of the legend and the printed column order of Tables 2 and 3.
  Both read the PDF's text layer, so an error in that layer would pass both.
- **A rendered-image check** reads no glyph inside a cell. Each page of Table 1 is rasterised
  (Poppler `pdftoppm`, which draws glyphs and vector paths), and every cell rectangle of the
  52 × 27 matrix, occupied or empty, is tested for ink. A cell is consistent when it has ink if
  and only if the extraction found a code in it. The rectangles themselves come from text-layer
  positions (the printed row numbers and column headers) that the second extraction also uses.
  The check catches a code missed by both extractions in an otherwise empty cell, which would
  look like a specimen without a code; it cannot see a code missing from a cell that holds
  another code, nor the identity of a code. Whether every occupied cell has more ink than any
  empty one is reported; the pixel margin depends on the rasteriser build and is written to the
  run log.
- **The article's prose** gives, for the museum specimens identified to species, each species
  with its number of specimens. That list is compared with the current identifications
  extracted from Table 3, a reading that shares nothing with the table parser.

Positive controls show that these checks detect a code shifted to the next column or row, two
swapped columns, a value read from another row or moved into the next field, a changed digit,
a missed or invented code, and a code erased from the rendered page itself. No check compares
a catalogue number with the museum's own catalogue; a mis-keyed digit is caught only if
it breaks the sequence of the numbers (see below).

### Canonical model and harmonisation

The canonical model is written in LinkML {{ cite("sw_linkml") }} as nine classes:
source document, reference, taxon, taxon-name variant, locality, occurrence, identification,
distribution assertion and habitat statement. Every slot that has a Darwin Core equivalent
declares it as `slot_uri`, and the schema is the single contract for column names, types,
required fields, patterns and permitted values. How each canonical field is produced is listed
in a source-to-canonical mapping table of {{ c.mapping_rows }} rows. Each row gives the source
column, canonical field, Darwin Core term and one of {{ c.rules }} named transformation rules.
A test keeps the table and the rule registry in the code identical in both directions.

Four modelling decisions matter for what follows.

**Taxa are name usages.** A taxon record is *a name as used in the article*
(`dwc:nameAccordingTo` the article), not a taxon concept. External identifiers are attached
later as mappings, so a name synonymised since then stays visible. For the literature records
the article reports, the graph holds the 2016 compilers' names; whether they updated the names
used in the original sources cannot be known from the article. Only species-group names are
resolved, together with one genus and one family used as identifications; higher ranks (such as
the order Zoanthidea of Table 1) are not. Open nomenclature ("cf.", "sp.") is kept in
`dwc:identificationQualifier`, and the base name is resolved.

**Specimens and Table 1 assertions are kept apart.** A code in Table 1 summarises evidence,
some of it specimens that are also listed in Tables 2 and 3. Treating each code as an
independent occurrence would count the same organism twice. Table 1 assertions therefore
become their own records, each with its own event. Their Darwin Core basis of record is
*MaterialCitation* for a museum code, which cites material, and *Occurrence* for the other codes,
which may rest on no specimen at all. An assertion traced to a listed specimen, including one the
article now identifies as another taxon or could not verify, points to it twice: with a project
property whose value is the specimen's IRI (`akv:citesSpecimen`), and with
`dwc:associatedOccurrences` written as "(cited specimen) MOHBR:I-…".

**Habitat belongs to the taxon.** The article describes habitat in each species account,
not per record, and copying it onto occurrences would invent per-record habitat.

**Judgement is curated, not coded.** Corrections that need judgement are rows in curated
tables, each with its evidence and the date of the author's review: {{ c.curated_rows }} rows in
{{ c.curated_files | words }} files (Additional file 3). They cover {{ c.typos | words }} misspellings of
names in Table 2 (e.g. *Phymantus* for *Phymanthus*), spelling variants of state names, links
from collecting sites to Table 1 localities, collection dates stated only in the Methods of
the article, decisions on fuzzy name matches, the habitat vocabulary, and the nomenclatural act
behind each name change of Table 4. Values that cannot be harmonised become nulls, and the
printed value is kept in a verbatim field.

**Dates keep their precision.** {{ d.by_id.W12 }} of the {{ e.t3_parseable }} valid dates of Table 3 fall on
day 01, against about {{ (e.t3_parseable / 30.4) | round | int }} expected if days were uniform, the pattern of a
defaulted day; these are published as year-month. The impossible date "31/04/2012" keeps its year.
What the date column of Appendix 2 denotes (collection, cataloguing or identification) is not
stated in the article. The printed date is always kept verbatim.

**Changes of identification are coded.** Where Table 3 gives a previous identification, the
reason for the change is classified against WoRMS: a spelling or gender ending only, a name
update with the same accepted name, a changed determination, or, for the specimens the article
lists as "not identified", an identification that "could not be corroborated", which is recorded
as unverified (`dwc:identificationVerificationStatus`), not as refuted.

Identifiers are deterministic UUIDv5 values. Records derived from the article use a namespace
derived from its DOI. Specimens use a namespace derived from the institution, and their natural
key is institution and catalogue number ("MOHBR:I-1576"), for the collected specimens as for the
museum ones, so that a lot keeps its identifier wherever it is cited later. The one collected
specimen without a number ("In progress") is keyed on its field code. No key includes the
identification. Each catalogue entry is modelled as one organism; the article does not say
whether an entry is an individual or a lot.

### Name resolution and habitat alignment

Each of the {{ r.n_taxa }} names was looked up in WoRMS {{ cite("worms2026") }}, the GBIF
Backbone Taxonomy {{ cite("gbif2026") }} and NCBI Taxonomy {{ cite("schoch2020") }}. Every
name receives one record per service, whether resolved or not, shaped after SSSOM
{{ cite("matentzoglu2022") }}: subject, predicate, object, justification, method, tool and date.
The policy is the same for all services:

- **Exact:** the service holds the printed name, at the same rank, and only once: within
  Cnidaria for WoRMS and GBIF, and in the whole of NCBI Taxonomy (`[Scientific Name]` class) for
  NCBI. The link is `skos:exactMatch` {{ cite("w3c_skos") }}; it carries no confidence value,
  only its method.
- **Homonyms:** where WoRMS holds several records for the same name, the record whose
  authority matches the authority printed in the article is kept, and the method says so. NCBI
  returned no ambiguous name. One name printed without an authority (*Ceriantheomorphe
  brasiliensis*) cannot be assigned to either of its WoRMS homonyms; its curated link is
  `skos:closeMatch`.
- **Fuzzy matches** become candidates and never become identifiers unless the author accepts
  them in a curated file. Each accepted variant records its case under the Code
  {{ cite("iczn1999") }}: mandatory gender agreement of an adjectival epithet (Art. 34.2), or an
  incorrect subsequent spelling (Art. 33.3; under Art. 33.4, replacing -i by -ii, or the reverse,
  in a genitive formed from a personal name is an incorrect subsequent spelling, not an
  emendation). Spellings were judged against the WoRMS records, not against the original
  descriptions, and whether a variant is in prevailing usage (Art. 33.3.1) was not assessed, so
  these decisions are provisional. A curated variant is linked with `skos:closeMatch`, in all three
  services alike. The match score is exported as an SSSOM similarity score, not as a confidence.
- **Accepted names:** for names that are not accepted, the record of the accepted name is
  retrieved as well, linked by `dwc:acceptedNameUsageID`.
- **NCBI fallback:** if NCBI holds no node under the printed name, the WoRMS accepted name is
  tried, including the accepted spelling of a curated variant. Such a link (`skos:closeMatch`,
  justification `semapv:MappingChaining`) joins the article's name usage to the NCBI node that
  carries the WoRMS accepted name; it makes no claim about circumscription. Its method records
  whether the chain crosses a genus change; a "no" is not reassurance, because a species-level
  synonymy is a claim about the species.

`skos:exactMatch` therefore joins only identical names. That matters because in SKOS it is
symmetric and transitive, and its subjects and objects are `skos:Concept`s: a reasoner that loads
the SKOS axioms closes every chain of exact links. Had curated variants been exact links, the
closure would have equated two WoRMS records of one name, one of them unaccepted, and two
article usages that Table 3 gives as the previous and current identification of one specimen.
A SHACL constraint now fails if the closure of exact links holds two records of one service.
`skos:closeMatch` is not transitive. Neither predicate licenses any inference about
circumscription, and the graph does not import the SKOS axioms.

Three further analyses describe what the links rest on. First, for every name whose WoRMS record
is not accepted, the sources WoRMS cites for the change (a new combination, a synonymy or a
taxonomy source) were retrieved. They are not necessarily the act, so for each change the act
that fixes today's accepted name was identified, where possible, and recorded in a curated table
with its year, the relation it creates and whether it changes a concept or only a name. Second,
every name unresolved in NCBI was queried again under all NCBI name classes, under its accepted
name and every WoRMS synonym of it, and at genus level. For each linked NCBI node, the nucleotide
records of the node and its descendants were counted in NCBI's nuccore database (which includes
RefSeq as well as INSDC records), and nuccore was searched for the strings "MOHBR" and "Hermano
Benigno" in all fields. GBIF datasets and the GRSciColl registry were searched for the
institution, to see whether its specimens are already published.
Third, the services were documented: the GBIF Backbone dataset
({{ f.prov.releases.gbif_backbone.dataset_key }}, published
{{ f.prov.releases.gbif_backbone.pub_date[:10] | longdate }} as served on the access date), NCBI
Taxonomy (accessed; its database reported an update on
{{ f.prov.releases.ncbi_taxonomy.last_update[:10] | replace("/", "-") | longdate }}), and ENVO release
{{ f.prov.releases.envo.version }}. WoRMS and NCBI publish no release numbers, so the access date
of each response is its version.

Habitat paragraphs were aligned to ENVO in two separate steps. First, a curated vocabulary of
{{ f.envo.vocab }} phrases maps wording in the article to ENVO class labels; this is a human
decision. Second, each label is resolved to an IRI through the EBI Ontology Lookup Service, by
exact label only. A phrase preceded by a negation within a few words is recorded as negated and
not annotated; the verbatim habitat text, which keeps the exclusion, is kept as `dwc:habitat`.

All {{ r.cached }} API responses ({{ r.cache_purpose.resolution }} for resolution and
{{ r.cache_purpose.coverage }} for the analyses above; retrieved {{ r.date_first | longdate }}–{{ r.date_last | longdate }})
are cached and committed with the code. Each mapping cites the cached response it was derived
from, and in offline mode, as in continuous integration, no request leaves the machine.

### Data quality as code

Data quality is checked before any RDF is produced. **Column-level checks** are generated from
the LinkML schema as pandera {{ cite("bantilan2020") }} schemas, giving {{ d.schema_checks }}
checks of type, presence, uniqueness, pattern, permitted values and range.

**Cross-table rules** cover what a single column cannot:

- **{{ d.n_critical }} critical rules** cover referential integrity, uniqueness of specimens and
  Table 1 assertions, exactly one current identification per specimen, coordinates given as a
  pair and inside a box around Venezuela's coast and islands (0.5–16° N, 59.5–73.5° W; no
  on-land test; the article states no datum), new-record codes only on survey records,
  consistency of mappings, closure of WoRMS accepted-name pointers, and catalogue numbers in
  sequence (strictly ascending in Table 3, consecutive in Table 2), so that a mis-keyed digit that
  stays unique is still caught. Any critical failure stops the pipeline.
- **{{ d.n_warning }} warnings** record incompleteness and disagreement between sources without
  changing anything.

Every rule, critical or warning, has a positive control: a test injects a fault and requires the
rule to report it.

**Comparing Table 1 with the specimen tables.** The unit is a cell: one WoRMS accepted name in
one Table 1 area, with both directions merged and every area included, not only those from which
the specimen tables have specimens. A cell whose taxon has no row in Table 1 (the family-level
*Actiniidae* sp. of the specimen tables) cannot disagree with it and is left out as not
comparable. A cell can show four kinds of discrepancy: a survey code without a Table 2 specimen
currently identified as that taxon; a museum code without such a Table 3 specimen; and the two
reverse cases. Causes are recorded as multiple labels, every one that applies, and each cell
falls in one class:

- *explained* by the article itself: Table 3 re-identified the specimen, or lists it as not
  identified;
- *possibly explained*: a specimen of the same family identified only above species level lies
  in the same area, in the table the missing specimen would be in. This can account for a code
  without a specimen, never for a specimen without a code;
- *differing only in the code*: the same presence appears under another code;
- *unexplained*: none of these.

An *uncertain link* is a curated link from a Table 3 locality to a Table 1 area that is neither
an exact name nor a printed park name ({{ x.uncertain_links }} links: {{ x.uncertain_links - f.loc.crosswalk_t3_rejected }} the curator
accepted, mostly a locality named inside an area such as "Cayo Sombrero, Morrocoy", and
{{ f.loc.crosswalk_t3_rejected | words }} the curator rejected). The class is syntactic: it says how a link was made, not how likely
it is to be wrong. Whether a discrepancy depends on such links is a condition, not an
explanation: the comparison and the evidence grading are re-run with the rejected links linked,
with the accepted ones unlinked, and with all of them unlinked or linked. For each rejected
link, the taxa of the Table 3 locality are compared with the museum codes of the candidate area,
evidence internal to the article that does not decide the link.

**Evidence tiers.** Every Table 1 assertion receives one tier, which depends on both
traceability and the identification status of the specimen behind it. A survey code may have a
Table 2 specimen of the taxon from the same area identified without qualifier, one identified
with cf. or sp., or none. A museum code may have a matching Table 3 specimen from the area, may be
traceable only to a specimen now identified as another taxon or listed as not identified, or
may be untraced; among these three, a code whose only specimen the article now calls something
else ranks lowest, because it is the article's own counter-evidence. A literature record's name
cannot be fixed to a current taxon concept. Matching is by WoRMS accepted name. "Survey" and
"museum" name how the material reached the collection, not whether it is vouchered: every
listed specimen carries a MOHBR number. All identifications are the source authors', made from
external morphology.

### Knowledge graph

The graph reuses existing vocabularies and mints a project term only where none fits.

- **Occurrences, organisms, events, identifications and taxa** use Darwin Core
  {{ cite("wieczorek2012") }}. Darwin-SW {{ cite("baskauf2016") }} links them: occurrence → event
  → location, occurrence → organism, identification → organism. WoRMS records carry
  `dwc:taxonomicStatus` from the GBIF vocabulary (accepted or synonym; other WoRMS statuses go to
  `dwc:nomenclaturalStatus` or `dwc:taxonRemarks`), `dwc:nameAccordingTo` with the access date,
  and `dwc:acceptedNameUsageID`. Locations comprise the areas and sites of the article and the
  four regions into which its Table 1 groups the areas; these are the article's labels, not
  ecoregions. The {{ c.sites_with_coords }} sites with printed coordinates carry
  `dwc:geodeticDatum` "not recorded" and the precision of the print; no uncertainty radius is
  invented.
- **Taxa** are linked to external records with SKOS rather than `owl:sameAs`, so that a name
  usage of 2016 is not merged with today's accepted name.
- **Each identifier link** is also reified as an SSSOM mapping record carrying its justification
  (SEMAPV), its method and evidence (SSSOM `comment`, whose IRI is `rdfs:comment`), a similarity
  score where fuzzy, the tool, its version and the date, and derived from the cached response,
  which holds the request, with PROV-O {{ cite("w3c_provo") }}. The SSSOM table also lists the
  rejected candidates (`predicate_modifier: Not`) and the names a service lacks
  (`sssom:NoTermFound`).
- **Every record** is `prov:wasDerivedFrom` the article's DOI. A qualified derivation gives
  the locator in the article, the page and the pipeline stage.

The {{ f.terms.total }} classes, properties and concepts the graph may use are listed in a term
registry, each verified against a cached copy of the vocabulary that defines it; the graph
builder refuses any other term. The project vocabulary has {{ f.terms.akv }} terms (Fig. 2),
including the evidence tier and two small concept schemes. The graph was built with RDFLib
{{ cite("sw_rdflib") }} and serialised as Turtle and JSON-LD.

