### Identifier resolution

WoRMS resolved all {{ r.worms.accepted }} names and GBIF {{ r.gbif.accepted }}. NCBI Taxonomy
resolved {{ r.ncbi.accepted - r.ncbi.via_accepted }} by exact name and {{ r.ncbi.via_accepted | words }}
more only through their WoRMS accepted name (Fig. 3, Table 3), {{ r.ncbi.via_variant | words }} of them the accepted
spelling of a curated variant; {{ e.ncbi_fallback_genus_change | words }} of these {{ r.ncbi.via_accepted | words }} cross a genus change,
so the NCBI node may not circumscribe what the article meant. The three services are not independent: the GBIF Backbone draws on WoRMS for marine
groups. The {{ r.n_taxa }} names matched {{ f.worms_nodes.matched }} WoRMS records, because two misspellings share
the record of their correct form. WoRMS held more than one record for
{{ r.homonyms_total | words }} of the names; for {{ r.homonyms_by_authority | words }} of them the printed authority picked the
record, for example *Zoanthus sociatus* (Ellis, 1768) rather than a *taxon inquirendum* of the
same name by Le Sueur (1818). The remaining name, *Ceriantheomorphe brasiliensis*, is printed
without an authority, and both of its records point to the same accepted name.

The fuzzy candidates show why fuzzy matches are never accepted automatically. GBIF matched the
historical label *Palythoa caribbeorum* to *Palythoa caribbea*, a different species, with a
score of 0.98. The author rejected that match and accepted WoRMS' match to *P. caribaeorum* as
an inferred incorrect subsequent spelling: Table 3 gives the two names as the previous and
current identification of the same specimen, which was not re-examined for this paper. All
{{ r.overrides | words }} curated decisions ({{ r.overrides_accept | words }} accepted, {{ r.overrides_reject | words }} rejected)
are rows with evidence, and the pipeline fails if any of them is never used.

