### What the distribution rests on

The {{ cl.n }} taxon × area cells of Table 1 (printed name × area) were graded by their best
evidence (Fig. 4). {{ cl.specimen }} ({{ cl.pct_specimen }}%) rest on a specimen listed from the area and identified
without qualifier by the source authors, from external morphology. {{ cl.survey_code }} rest on a survey code
for a species vouchered from another area, {{ cl.museum_code }} on a museum code without a matching
specimen, and {{ cl.survey_never | words }} on a survey code for a species never vouchered. {{ cl.lit_only }} ({{ cl.pct_lit_only }}%) rest
on literature only, of which {{ cl.agudo_alone }} ({{ cl.pct_agudo_alone }}%) cite one monograph and nothing else
{{ cite("agudo1987") }}; a cell citing that monograph and another source counts as "other". Counted in
codes rather than cells, the monograph accounts for {{ f.codes.agudo }} of the {{ f.codes.literature }} literature codes
({{ f.codes.pct_agudo_of_literature }}%). It is cited through the article and was not consulted here. Some of its
records do rest on specimens, since the article re-examined "those from Agudo (1987) vouchered at
the MOHBR", but which ones, and what its names denote today, is unknown. The specimen share
depends on the linking choices: from {{ cl.scenario_share.all_unlinked }}% with all uncertain links unlinked to
{{ cl.scenario_share.all_linked }}% with all linked (Table 6).

The split between the {{ cl.visited_areas }} areas the authors visited and the other {{ (cl.areas_per_region.values() | sum) - cl.visited_areas }} describes
where evidence exists, not how sampling changes its quality. In the visited areas
{{ cl.visited.pct_specimen }}% of the cells rest on a specimen from the area and {{ cl.visited.pct_lit_only }}% on literature only; in the
others, {{ cl.not_visited.pct_specimen }}% and {{ cl.not_visited.pct_lit_only }}%. The strata differ partly by construction: a survey
code can only occur in a visited area, so there the specimen can come from either table,
elsewhere only from the museum. The museum route alone gives {{ cl.museum_route.visited.confirmed }} of
{{ cl.museum_route.visited.codes }} museum codes with a matching specimen in visited areas and
{{ cl.museum_route.not_visited.confirmed }} of {{ cl.museum_route.not_visited.codes }} elsewhere. The visited areas also hold more cells each
({{ (cl.visited.n / cl.visited_areas) | round | int }} against {{ (cl.not_visited.n / ((cl.areas_per_region.values() | sum) - cl.visited_areas)) | round | int }}), and they are unevenly spread among the regions
