## Discussion

**Keeping the publication intact.** The central design choice was to treat the article as data
to be preserved, not as text to be corrected. Names are kept as name usages and linked to
current records with SKOS and SSSOM rather than replaced, so the 2016 inventory and today's
taxonomy coexist in one graph, each with its own provenance (Table 4). Provider disagreements
about circumscription, such as NCBI's treatment of *Bunodeopsis antilliensis*, are reported in
Additional file 4 rather than encoded in the graph. Keeping the specimen tables apart from the
distribution matrix is what made the cross-table comparison possible.

**Judgement where it is needed, code everywhere else.** Most of the work is deterministic and
tested: extraction and its checks, rule-based harmonisation, exact matching, validation. The
decisions that need expertise fill {{ c.curated_rows }} rows in {{ c.curated_files | words }} curated files. Each carries
its evidence and the date it was reviewed; a decision that no longer applies is detected,
because the pipeline fails on an unused override; and the mapping justification says whether a
machine or a person asserted a link. It also makes the decisions auditable by someone other
than the curator, which matters here because the curator is a co-author of the source.

**Reading the cross-table differences.** Read per cell, {{ x.explained + x.code_level_only }} of the {{ x.cells }} differences between
the matrix and the specimen tables have a firm cause in the article itself or are differences of
coding rather than of presence, and {{ x.possible | words }} more possibly does. Of the {{ x.unexplained }} that remain,
{{ x.unexplained_by_kind.survey_code_no_voucher }} are survey codes without a specimen from the area, which the legend does not promise
and the article's design (vouchers per species) leads one to expect. The {{ x.unexplained_by_kind.museum_code_no_specimen | words }} museum codes
without a matching specimen in Appendix 2 look like the clearest residual, since the legend
refers code "a" to that appendix; but most of them sit in two areas whose Table 3 localities the
curator declined to link, and at Laguna de Piedras the taxa of the unlinked locality match the
codes one for one. The residual is therefore mostly a question of locality names, which the
source authors or the museum can settle, and the list is offered to them. These are differences
between tables, not demonstrated errors.

**Fitness for use.** The graph is suitable as a literature-based checklist with graded evidence:
for tracing each record to its evidence, choosing records by evidence tier, and reconciling the
2016 names with current taxonomy. It is not, as it stands, suitable for species distribution
models or for area-of-occupancy estimates in red-list assessments. Only {{ c.sites_with_coords }} sites carry source
coordinates, of unstated datum; the {{ f.loc.areas }} areas range from a single beach to a gulf and include
lagoons and gulfs whose salinity regime is not recorded; literature records have no date; and the
species accounts give depth ranges that were not extracted. Presence is reported without absence
or effort, beyond the flag on the six visited localities. The article does not state whether any
species is introduced, and no establishment means is recorded. Nor is the graph an
expected-species list for environmental DNA: it carries no event-level environment, and it
lists largely benthic, mostly sessile taxa for which eDNA shedding, and the assignment of reads
to host or symbiont, are open questions.

**Publishing the records.** The two classes of occurrence need different routes. The
{{ c.occurrence }} specimens can go to GBIF and OBIS as occurrences, keyed on the museum's catalogue
numbers, once a georeference layer exists and the museum agrees: no GBIF dataset or GRSciColl
entry for the MOHBR invertebrate collection was found, so they would not duplicate one. The
{{ c.distribution }} Table 1 assertions, undated and located only by area name, should not: they belong
in a GBIF checklist with a distribution extension (area, occurrence status, source, and the
evidence tier in its remarks). OBIS indexes occurrence and event datasets, so for OBIS the
assertions would need their own occurrence dataset, flagged as literature-derived. The link from
an assertion to its specimen crosses datasets, and Darwin Core carries it as a resource
relationship, or as `associatedReferences` to the specimen dataset's DOI; the graph's
`akv:citesSpecimen` holds it until then.

**Limitations.** Nine limitations apply.

- **One article.** The parser relies on the layout of this article and refuses unexpected
  layouts, and the SHACL shapes encode its identifier formats. Only the layers after extraction
  are expected to transfer, with a shape profile per source.
- **Curation by a co-author.** The {{ c.curated_rows }} curated rows were made and reviewed by a
  co-author of the source. An independent audit is required before any journal submission
  (Next steps).
- **What the extraction checks cover.** The two extractions read the same text layer and share
  layout knowledge; the rendered-image check shares the cell geometry and covers presence in
  otherwise empty cells of Table 1; no check compares a catalogue number with the museum.
- **Identifications.** All are the source authors', from external morphology, which is
  insufficient for several zoanthid and actiniarian genera; "unqualified" is not "verified".
- **Habitat per species.** Habitat is attached to species as the article reports it; half of the
  names have none, ENVO lacks a class for coral rubble, and biotic associations are not modelled.
- **No georeferencing.** Areas are kept as named, without datum, salinity regime or depth.
- **Heuristic scores.** The scores of fuzzy matches only ordered candidates for review.
- **Placeholder namespaces.** The namespaces are not yet registered, so the graph's IRIs do not
  dereference (nor do WoRMS LSIDs, which carry a web link), and the repository has no archival
  DOI yet.
- **A dated snapshot.** Resolution reflects the external services on the access dates.

**Next steps**, in order:

1. **An independent audit, as a condition for journal submission.** Its items are the
   {{ c.curated_rows }} curated rows and the decisions written in code: open nomenclature resolved to the
   base name, homonyms chosen by authority, spellings judged against WoRMS, the relations and
   acts of Table 4, and the treatment of higher ranks. Auditors must not be authors of the
   source article, of the acts assessed, or editors of the WoRMS records used. They work from
   the evidence column, blind to the curator's decision; each item passes when the auditor's
   decision agrees, disagreements are recorded with their reasons in a versioned file, and the
   rate of disagreement is reported. A name audit cannot verify the identifications themselves:
   that needs the specimens, and for zoanthids molecular markers.
2. **Questions for the source authors and the museum**, kept apart from the audit: whether
   Laguna de Punta de Piedras is the Table 1 Laguna de Piedras, and Golfo de Cariaco La Chica;
   what the date column of Appendix 2 records; what the I-series numbers and a catalogue entry
   (individual or lot) denote; and the two species without a voucher.
3. An archival deposit with a DOI, and registered namespaces.
4. A derived georeference layer (point-radius with coordinate uncertainty, datum, Marine Regions
   identifiers and a Marine Ecoregions of the World assignment {{ cite("spalding2007") }}, and a
   salinity or estuarine-zone attribute for the lagoons and gulfs), before any publication.
5. Publication: specimens as occurrences, Table 1 as a checklist with a distribution extension.
6. Biotic associations as interactions, and crosswalks from the habitat vocabulary to CMECS and
   the IUCN Habitats Classification Scheme; a request for an ENVO class for coral rubble.
7. Molecular and morphological data joined to the same specimens through their catalogue
   numbers.
8. Periodic re-resolution, reporting changes in Table 4 and in NCBI coverage.

## Conclusions

A species inventory published as a PDF can be made machine-actionable without rewriting it.
In this case:

- every one of the {{ s.t1_codes }} distribution codes and {{ s.t2 + s.t3 }} specimen records is traceable
  to its page, table and row; a second extraction agrees with every one of them, and a check of
  the rendered pages confirms the presence of a code in every occupied cell of Table 1;
- every identifier link records how, when and by whom it was made;
- the graph has no SHACL violation and is rebuilt byte for byte from its committed inputs.

The process graded the inventory's evidence. Only {{ cl.pct_specimen }}% of its taxon × area cells
({{ cl.scenario_share.all_unlinked | round | int }}–{{ cl.scenario_share.all_linked | round | int }}% across linking choices) rest on a specimen from the area, and
{{ cl.pct_lit_only }}% rest on literature alone, most of them on one monograph. Its three tables differ in
{{ x.cells }} comparable cells; once re-identifications, coding differences, the one possible coarse
identification and the survey codes that the legend does not tie to a specimen are set aside,
{{ x.unexplained - x.unexplained_by_kind.survey_code_no_voucher }} remain, {{ x.unexplained_link_dependent | words }} of them conditional on locality links the curator rejected.
Reading any one table of the article shows none of this. The result is a checklist with graded
evidence, not a basis for distribution models or red-list area estimates.

